.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SS "template<uint32_t dim>
.br
class csmp::Face< dim >"Lower dimensional surface (in 3D) or line (2D) elements serve as boundaries (\fBFace\fP) or connect disconnected mesh domains (\fBInterFace\fP)\&. The \fBFace\fP is used for material interfaces that are welded together by sharing nodes\&. \fBBoundary\fP objects of a CSMP model consist of \fBFace\fP objects\&.

.PP
The \fBFace\fP is rather similar in its functionality to the \fBElement\fP with many member functions sharing their names\&. As an important difference, \fBFace\fP objects know their higher-dimensional neighbor \fBElement\fP objects\&. This means that \fBFace\fP objects are not only connected to each other, but have an extra set of pointers that connect them to \fBElement\fP objects\&.

.PP
\fBFace\fP objects also have a unit normal that helps with the application of tractions etc\&. Since the \fBFace\fP is not inherited from \fBElement\fP, it constitutes a separate type and can store unique variables, especially ones like tractions, influxes or transfer coefficients, which would unnecessarily consume storage if discretised evenly over a domain\&. \fBElement\fP variables like permeability can still be accessed easily because the \fBFace\fP has pointers to its inner and outer \fBElement\fP neighbors\&.

.PP
\fBFace\fP objects have been designed to support operations on internal or external model boundaries\&. If a \fBFace\fP is located at an external (\fBModel\fP) boundary its normal will point outward and only the inner higher-dimensional neighbor 0 will be initialised\&. Representing internal boundaries, \fBFace\fP objects provide access to both neighbors, also helping to distinguish inside from outside domains\&. This distinction is possible and indicated by to the \fBFace\fP normal that points to the outside\&. Thus, the \fBElement\fP objects that the \fBFace\fP normal points to are referred to as outside and the ones on the opposite side are the internal ones\&. This also means that the sense of node numbering of the \fBFace\fP matches that of the face of the \fBElement\fP on the inside\&.

.PP
\fBNote\fP
.RS 4
without a topology it is impossible to "compute" what the inside or outside of a surface in a boundary representation is\&. This info comes from CAD or geomodel into CSMP++ which can only perform consistency checks\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
Since so much information is required to construct complete \fBFace\fP objects, this is done in 2 steps: 1) the various constructors establish \fBFace\fP connectivity with higher-dimensional neighbors, associated indices and policies\&. 2) separate operations assign idx, nodes, neighbors, etc\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBAuthor\fP
.RS 4
Stephan Matthai 
.RE
